1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,312
The Science of Stretching.

2
00:00:02,262 --> 00:00:08,487
Stretching is one of the most widely recommended yet frequently misunderstood components of physical fitness.

3
00:00:08,487 --> 00:00:17,525
Research published in The Journal of Physiology has demonstrated that regular stretching produces measurable physiological changes beyond simply lengthening muscle tissue.

4
00:00:17,525 --> 00:00:31,275
A 2024 systematic review in the British Journal of Sports Medicine found that consistent stretching programs improved range of motion by an average of 18 percent over eight weeks, with sustained benefits for joint health and injury prevention.

5
00:00:31,275 --> 00:00:35,387
Two primary types of stretching dominate the research literature.

6
00:00:35,387 --> 00:00:41,600
Static stretching involves holding a position at the end range of motion for 15 to 60 seconds.

7
00:00:41,600 --> 00:00:47,575
Dynamic stretching, by contrast, uses controlled movement through a joint's full range.

8
00:00:47,575 --> 00:01:04,237
A landmark study in the Scandinavian Journal of Medicine and Science in Sports showed that dynamic stretching before athletic activity reduced injury rates by 35 percent compared to static stretching protocols, while post-exercise static stretching aided in recovery by reducing muscle stiffness.

9
00:01:04,237 --> 00:01:07,912
The mechanisms underlying these benefits are complex.

10
00:01:07,912 --> 00:01:14,875
Stretching triggers mechanotransduction, a process where mechanical force is converted into cellular signals.

11
00:01:14,875 --> 00:01:23,350
This stimulates the production of collagen and increases sarcomere number in muscle fibers, effectively allowing muscles to tolerate greater lengthening.

12
00:01:23,350 --> 00:01:36,875
The NIH National Institute of Arthritis and Musculoskeletal and Skin Diseases notes that consistent stretching also improves blood flow to soft tissues and reduces neuromuscular excitability, which lowers the risk of muscle tears.

13
00:01:36,875 --> 00:01:50,100
Emerging research from the American College of Sports Medicine indicates that stretching programs tailored to specific muscle groups, such as the hamstrings or hip flexors, produce greater functional improvements than generalized routines.

14
00:01:50,100 --> 00:01:59,575
The evidence supports a balanced approach: dynamic stretching before activity and static stretching afterward, performed at least three times per week for optimal results.

